{"candidates": [{"claim": "The safest protocol is an append-only intent-and-seal log with epoch fencing; it dominates lease-based alternatives because it records causality directly rather than inferring it from timeouts, and the exact durable records are the intent entry and the seal entry.", "mechanism": "Before acquiring the workspace, the process appends an intent record (problem_id, epoch, workspace_path, process_id) to a durable append-only log. Upon completion, it appends a seal record containing the output evidence hash. Reclamation performs a log-scan: any workspace with an intent but no matching seal, whose holding process has been confirmed dead by a liveness oracle, is reclaimed. The causal mechanism is that the log provides a recoverable total order of intent and seal events, so displacement is always preceded by a visible gap (intent without seal), never silent. Adjudication against lease protocols: where the evidence shows lease expiry can race with seal writes, the log protocol wins because seal visibility is immediate to readers of the log, whereas lease expiry is a timeout inference.", "counterconditions": ["The log is subject to unbounded growth if seal records are never compacted", "If the log service is partitioned, the protocol stalls rather than reclaims", "A process that writes an intent but crashes before touching the workspace creates a phantom reclamation candidate that wastes space until TTL"], "typicality": 0.92, "optional_refs": ["SRC_001"], "sidecar": {"search_signal": "productive", "requested_context_aliases": []}}, {"claim": "A content-addressed derivation cache with durable provenance anchors achieves equivalent safety without requiring a global log, because evidence identity is decoupled from workspace location; the exact durable record is the (problem_id, input_hash, output_hash) provenance anchor.", "mechanism": "The deterministic workspace implies reproducibility. The protocol durably records a provenance anchor mapping problem_i