"requested_context_aliases": []}}, {"claim": "Crash-only software with resurrection logs: safety is achieved by never distinguishing between crash and restart; the durable record is a birth certificate that enables idempotent workspace reconstruction rather than hazardous reclamation.", "mechanism": "On every startup, the process writes a birth certificate (problem_id, deterministic_seed, workspace_path, incarnation_number) to durable storage. All writes to the workspace are idempotent and logged in a redo log. 'Reclamation' is simply a new process incarnation reading the birth certificate and replaying the log to determine if evidence was already completed. If completed, the workspace is re-adopted; if not, it is reconstructed. The causal mechanism is deterministic replay: the workspace is not a resource to reclaim but a function of the durable birth certificate and log. This protocol never displaces evidence because there is no separate reclamation step—only continuation. Adjudication: this dominates both log and lease protocols when determinism holds, by eliminating the orphan-detection problem entirely.", "counterconditions": ["Deterministic replay must be total; any external I/O or nondeterminism invalidates the guarantee", "Log replay cost may be prohibitive for large workspaces", "Requires the workspace storage to be content-addressed or journaled to avoid corruption between incarnations"], "typicality": 0.65, "optional_refs": ["SRC_001"], "sidecar": {"search_signal": "productive", "requested_context_aliases": []}}, {"claim": "An epistemic, data-centric protocol using a distributed Merkle witness tree shifts safety from process liveness to content acknowledgment; the exact durable records are Merkle roots and inclusion proofs.", "mechanism": "Rather than tracking process liveness, the protocol requires that any completed evidence be hashed and that hash inserted into a Merkle tree witnessed by multiple observers. Reclamation performs a 'corpse examination': i